{
  "passage": "The Western Ghats are a chain of mountains running parallel to the western coast of India, for about 1,600 kilometres, from Gujarat to Kerala and Tamil Nadu. They are older than the Himalayas and are one of the richest regions of the world for plants and animals. Scientists call such places biodiversity hotspots. The Western Ghats receive heavy rain from the south-west monsoon, and this supports thick evergreen forests on their western slopes. Many of the species living there are endemic, which means they are found nowhere else on Earth. The lion-tailed macaque, a monkey with a silver mane, and the Nilgiri tahr, a wild goat that lives on grassy hilltops, are two examples. Many frogs and flowering plants of the region are also endemic. The Neelakurinji, a shrub that covers the hills of Munnar in blue flowers, blooms only once in twelve years. The Western Ghats are also the source of many important rivers, such as the Godavari, the Krishna and the Kaveri, which supply water to millions of people in southern India. But these forests are under threat from mining, new roads, dams and the spread of plantations. Because of their importance, UNESCO declared the Western Ghats a World Heritage Site in 2012.",
